WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.740
today I'm going to be speaking with filmmaker
and journalist Daniel Lumbroso, whose debut

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:12.890
feature, White Noise, is based on his four
years reporting inside the alt right and opened

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:13.890
last year.

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:15.940
Daniel, so great to talk to you today.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:17.280
Thank you for having me on the show.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:24.520
So in the film, you spend time with at least
some folks that I know to some degree.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:30.410
I've interviewed Richard Spencer a few years
ago, and it was sort of an interesting experience,

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:31.410
which we can talk about.

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:34.840
I mean, I guess first to kind of set the stage,
what was the goal here?

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:36.360
How did you get into covering the alt?

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:37.360
Right.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:42.280
And how did you end up basically meeting a
lot of these people and spending time with

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:43.280
them?

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:46.750
So I was one of the first reporters in the
country to start covering the right right

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:50.131
around when actually you were putting out
these really important primers on the movement.

00:00:50.131 --> 00:00:52.570
And I was watching them at the time.

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:56.300
If you go back to twenty sixteen, when Trump
was a candidate, the movement was not getting

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:57.320
a lot of attention.

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.730
And I was a young reporter at the Atlantic
at the time.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:04.729
And, you know, I just said to my editors,
like, there is this fiercely anti-Semitic,

00:01:04.729 --> 00:01:08.880
racist movement that's bubbling up online
at the time on Reddit and obviously 4chan

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:09.880
as well.

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:14.100
I had only graduated from college a few years
earlier, so I saw know aspects of that on

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.929
college campuses already.

00:01:15.929 --> 00:01:18.729
And I just said to the editor of The Atlantic,
this is something we have to pay attention

00:01:18.729 --> 00:01:19.729
to.

00:01:19.729 --> 00:01:21.060
It's only growing in strength.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:26.180
A few months later, I started covering Richard
Spencer before he was a household name, and

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:29.669
I was the one who actually caught a roomful
of people breaking out into Nazi salutes.

00:01:29.669 --> 00:01:32.039
You might remember that viral video at the
time.

00:01:32.039 --> 00:01:33.039
Yep.

00:01:33.039 --> 00:01:38.259
And that was really important because it clarified
that this wasn't just a group of edgy conservatives

00:01:38.259 --> 00:01:40.060
like the way it was covered.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:43.109
And I think you also did a great job of clarifying
that at the time.

00:01:43.109 --> 00:01:47.380
This was fundamentally a white nationalist
movement and it needed to be covered with

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:50.450
the utmost due diligence and care.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:54.099
When Charlottesville happened about eight
months later, the Atlantic understood that

00:01:54.099 --> 00:01:57.889
this was only growing in strength and they
gave me the space to to produce a feature

00:01:57.889 --> 00:01:58.889
film.

00:01:58.889 --> 00:02:01.090
So that's when it really became a full time
project.

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:07.450
When you look at your time spent with these
folks and I know I know you've covered Richard

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:11.350
Spencer, you've covered Mike Synovate, you've
covered Lauren Southern and and many others

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:12.769
there.

00:02:12.769 --> 00:02:17.969
I'm curious your thoughts on whether we can
make any more general assessments of the goals

00:02:17.969 --> 00:02:22.599
of these folks, because to some degree, I
feel like many of them recognize that their

00:02:22.599 --> 00:02:27.350
goals, at least as stated, are not likely
to become a reality any time soon.

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:33.829
And so that leads me to wonder about whether
this is essentially a marketing of some kind.

00:02:33.829 --> 00:02:38.040
Marketing themselves presents essentially
becoming known within a movement and less

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:43.099
less about actually achieving the movement's
goals or whether or whatever.

00:02:43.099 --> 00:02:46.420
I mean, I guess that's that's I'll leave it
there.

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:49.440
It's a great question, I mean, it really runs
the gamut.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.709
I think fundamentally this is a violent movement.

00:02:51.709 --> 00:02:53.090
Richard Spencer, who coined the term.

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:54.450
All right.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:56.120
He believes in a wide ethno state.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:03:00.150
What that might sound like a flashy word,
but what it means is a country that has, let's

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:02.700
say, 90 percent white population.

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:06.150
The US is sixty three percent white and declining
every year.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:07.150
I think that's great.

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:10.739
We should not that we cheer for that, but
it's great to have a multicultural country

00:03:10.739 --> 00:03:15.190
where everyone gets along, you know, to get
to the 90 percent white ethnic state.

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:16.920
We're talking about mass deportations.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:18.859
We're talking about ethnic cleansing.

00:03:18.859 --> 00:03:21.060
Those are fundamentally racist ideas.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:22.379
But then you get the other side of it.

00:03:22.379 --> 00:03:26.209
And white noise really is all about the spectrum
of the right and how it goes.

00:03:26.209 --> 00:03:28.180
All the GRIF tourism, that's part of it.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:32.689
You get a guy like Mike Synovate who, you
know, is happy to benefit from the racism

00:03:32.689 --> 00:03:34.219
and how it can help you build a brand.

00:03:34.219 --> 00:03:37.870
And you see over the course of the film that
he's selling skin care products, he's selling

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:44.139
pills, he's making money off of big parties
and some crappy bar in New York City.

00:03:44.139 --> 00:03:45.780
There is there is so much about it.

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:46.780
That's opportunism.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:48.530
It's celebrity, it's vanity.

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:49.770
So the motivations are mixed.

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:53.329
And I think that's why people are having a
lot of having trouble sometimes making sense

00:03:53.329 --> 00:03:54.329
of what happened Wednesday.

00:03:54.329 --> 00:03:56.499
They're saying, is this serious or is it laughing?

00:03:56.499 --> 00:03:57.500
It can be both.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:03:58.629
And I found that it was both.

00:03:58.629 --> 00:03:59.629
Yeah.

00:03:59.629 --> 00:04:03.499
I mean, it seems like in a country of three
hundred and thirty one million, there's plenty

00:04:03.499 --> 00:04:09.969
of room for all of the above to be examples
of it and to still have plenty of everything.

00:04:09.969 --> 00:04:14.889
Exactly, and I think what needs to be clear,
though, is that even if you're coming from

00:04:14.889 --> 00:04:19.470
it as someone who's laughing, who's playing,
who's having their moment in the sun, you're

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:24.580
still adjusting yourself to the Spencers,
to the Oath Keepers, to these people who believe

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:25.660
this is a white man's country.

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:28.810
And I think that's morally problematic.

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:31.599
And maybe that will get you some more Twitter
followers and some money.

00:04:31.599 --> 00:04:34.180
But it's not worth the coalition that you're
in.

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:40.680
One of the things that's come up in the days
since the January 6th, riotous insurrection

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:46.240
is some even people I respect, like, for example,
economist Richard Wolffe, who was my college

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:52.870
professor and others have been sort of reducing
what took place last week to economic oppression

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:55.870
and the natural result of inequality.

00:04:55.870 --> 00:04:59.350
And I respect a lot of the folks that are
saying this, but I think that these are sort

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:01.479
of some of their less good takes.

00:05:01.479 --> 00:05:04.560
And I did a commentary about it earlier on
in today's program.

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:11.690
I'm curious because you you were in and observing
a lot of these movements.

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:15.490
What can we say about the degree to which
economic oppression plays a role?

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:20.050
Not for people like Spencer, who I believe
is from a wealthy family individually, but

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:22.129
in the people that followed these movements.

00:05:22.129 --> 00:05:27.880
To what degree could it be that economic oppression
is what makes them liable to get sucked in,

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:29.720
but is not really the focus of the activism?

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:32.670
Or what can we say about the economic aspect?

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:35.620
I think the economic anxiety argument is nonsense.

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:40.819
No offense to the professor, I mean, most
of the people that I covered from upper middle

00:05:40.819 --> 00:05:46.260
class, highly, highly educated, they live
in New York, D.C., Los Angeles.

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:52.080
All the scenes in my film take place in liberal
enclaves, San Francisco, Southern California,

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:53.340
D.C., many others.

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:55.560
I mean, that's where these movements have
strength.

00:05:55.560 --> 00:06:00.889
And, of course, they may attract footsoldiers
from Alabama and whatever, but this is not

00:06:00.889 --> 00:06:03.099
like your grandaddy's racism.

00:06:03.099 --> 00:06:06.270
People in you know, in trailer parks like
burning crosses.

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:08.960
And of course, there's some of that iconography.

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:15.230
But this is the leaders down to the followers
are really like smart, educated kids who have

00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:17.099
been radicalized by these ideas.

00:06:17.099 --> 00:06:18.909
And you ask about how it happens.

00:06:18.909 --> 00:06:23.720
It's not because they're hit with high taxes
and they feel like it's it's tyrannical and

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:25.690
they just want a more libertarian state.

00:06:25.690 --> 00:06:28.830
Maybe there are a few of those people I met,
very few of them.

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:33.659
What it really is, is that these people are
selling you on a promise that you might be

00:06:33.659 --> 00:06:38.280
a bored, unemployed kid in your mom's basement,
but you're actually a descendant of a crusader,

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:43.560
of a Greek, of a Roman that you could take
part in this amazing historical legacy.

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:50.550
And I think that's a really intoxicating narrative
to see that you're not just, you know, a disaffected

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:52.039
kid with no sense of purpose in the world.

00:06:52.039 --> 00:06:54.039
You could take part in something larger.

00:06:54.039 --> 00:06:56.949
And that's what they're really appealing to,
is a sense of narrative, kind of the sense

00:06:56.949 --> 00:06:58.710
of purpose, existential purpose.

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:06.220
Well, when I when I've interviewed former
KKK neo-Nazi types, Frank Mink and Scott Shepard

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:10.330
and, you know, a lot of these different interesting
people and those who have spent time with

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:16.509
them, folks like Daryl Davis as an example,
the thing that invariably comes up is that

00:07:16.509 --> 00:07:23.599
they were pulled into these movements from
a very particular circumstance, which almost

00:07:23.599 --> 00:07:30.470
always is lack of connection to other people,
often parents, but not necessarily feeling

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:34.430
out of touch with anything that they would
call community.

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:38.130
Sometimes there's abuse, psychological or
physical, that's going on.

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:41.850
And for the most part, they will they will
recognize these are the ones that made it

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:42.850
out.

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:43.850
Right.

00:07:43.850 --> 00:07:46.639
So it's a self-selecting group, but they will
recognize that the ideology could have almost

00:07:46.639 --> 00:07:51.500
been anything from whoever kind of presented
them with empathy and connection in a place

00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:53.830
where they could belong.

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:57.830
Can you talk about that a little bit in the
experiences you've had researching and doing

00:07:57.830 --> 00:07:58.830
the film?

00:07:58.830 --> 00:07:59.830
It's a great point.

00:07:59.830 --> 00:08:03.810
I mean, when you're inside the movement, it
feels like a subculture that could be any

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:04.870
kind of subculture.

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:08.069
It's almost like they're Internet celebrities
who happen to be racist.

00:08:08.069 --> 00:08:10.720
And of course, the racism is important.

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:15.360
But what you notice immediately is, is the
commonalities, the code of communication.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:19.720
They all dress the same 20th century like
Hitler Youth style haircuts.

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:24.610
Many of Richard Spencer's followers wear short
shorts and they're all drinking the same drinks

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:25.610
like whiskey.

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:27.750
They're all eating the same foods.

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:29.240
There's all of these ingroup jokes.

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:30.710
I mean, it's really a community.

00:08:30.710 --> 00:08:32.790
It's a cult like any other.

00:08:32.790 --> 00:08:37.310
And I've done a lot of embedded reporting
and other types of fundamentalist movements,

00:08:37.310 --> 00:08:39.220
but any any sort of community.

00:08:39.220 --> 00:08:43.950
And it resembles any sort of tight knit community
where the community is conferring importance

00:08:43.950 --> 00:08:47.240
onto the followers, giving them a sense of
purpose in the world.

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:52.580
And I think if you track it to the individual,
all of them have a sense of resentment.

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:55.100
They feel like they haven't been given their
due in the world.

00:08:55.100 --> 00:09:00.380
So like Smirnova, which was an out of work
lawyer, his first wife was very successful.

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:03.820
She's a she's a lawyer at several big tech
companies.

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:04.820
And they got divorced.

00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:09.080
He took a lot of her money and took a lot
of money and alimony, which is very, you know,

00:09:09.080 --> 00:09:11.840
Beita for the so-called alpha male.

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:14.810
And he felt really resentful that he never
really took off as a lawyer.

00:09:14.810 --> 00:09:16.410
And then he found a niche in this world.

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:21.900
He became someone prestigious on the far right,
you know, mainstreaming everything from pizza,

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:22.900
gay to that.

00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:24.130
Hillary had Parkinson's.

00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:26.020
He became a person of status.

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:28.120
And I think that's what this can provide.

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:29.420
And the list goes on and on.

00:09:29.420 --> 00:09:31.880
Richard Spencer was in and out of work.

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:33.060
Journalist never got any attention.

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:37.700
And then one day Trump ran was the gasoline
that Spencer needed to start the engine.

00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:41.990
And all of them, especially the leadership,
share that kind of story where the movement

00:09:41.990 --> 00:09:44.550
gave them I gave them a purpose in the world.

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:47.410
Not that this was the most important thing
you just said, but it just kind of piqued

00:09:47.410 --> 00:09:48.410
my curiosity.

00:09:48.410 --> 00:09:49.970
You say they eat the same foods.

00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:51.200
What foods are they eating?

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:55.130
That that's that's interesting.

00:09:55.130 --> 00:09:57.350
You know, there's that there is a few funny
examples.

00:09:57.350 --> 00:10:00.840
Lawrence Sutherland, who's one of the three
characters in the film and I think probably

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:04.500
the most interesting her has her her boyfriend
at the time.

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:05.890
They've now broken up.

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:10.330
We joked with a culinary white supremacist
because he told me he refused to eat, quote,

00:10:10.330 --> 00:10:12.000
non European foods.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:13.000
Really?

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:17.460
So I went I filmed them on a date once and
he wouldn't touch the ketchup because he claimed

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:22.520
ketchup was originally derived from China,
from the word ketchup or something.

00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:24.080
So he wouldn't touch the ketchup.

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:26.330
And we ate it like a British pub in Toronto.

00:10:26.330 --> 00:10:27.690
They ate.

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:28.690
It's in the film.

00:10:28.690 --> 00:10:31.580
And it's also in an article I wrote about
Lauren and that section of the story when.

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:33.790
Totally viral on Twitter because it's just
absurd.

00:10:33.790 --> 00:10:34.940
Yeah, that's interesting.

00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:41.340
Like, I wonder, is brisket considered white,
Russian or Jewish, like wood brisket be acceptable?

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:43.390
I wonder where they would come down on that.

00:10:43.390 --> 00:10:46.590
Well, Jews are not part of the ethnic state.

00:10:46.590 --> 00:10:47.590
Right.

00:10:47.590 --> 00:10:48.590
I'm Jewish.

00:10:48.590 --> 00:10:51.560
And I was on a road trip with with Spencer
to one of his shoots.

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:52.780
And the car broke down once.

00:10:52.780 --> 00:10:57.180
And we were stuck in central Florida and pitch
black at a gas station for a few hours.

00:10:57.180 --> 00:11:00.000
And I said, Richard, like, can I be a part
of your ethnic state?

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:03.720
And he was like, I'm sorry, Daniel, but it's
not going to work.

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:07.300
Well, it's interesting you mention that, because
when I had him on, there was this question

00:11:07.300 --> 00:11:11.980
of sort of like, you know, a lot of this idea
of the ethno state is based on some fundamental

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:16.280
incompatibility of these groups that just
like we can kind of pretend to get along.

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:20.480
And he said you and I could probably get together
and have a beer and there would not be an

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:24.130
obvious conflict in that particular interaction.

00:11:24.130 --> 00:11:27.920
But at some bigger picture level, there's
differences in values, there's differences

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:30.270
in culture, et cetera.

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:33.400
And and I don't know if he this interview
was three and a half years ago.

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:38.870
I don't know if it's he or others of his ilk
who have said that when Jews appear to be

00:11:38.870 --> 00:11:45.700
integrated into American culture, it's because
they've essentially given up whatever is core

00:11:45.700 --> 00:11:49.280
to them and are kind of adopting the American
thing.

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:51.660
And there's kind of these post hoc explanations.

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:56.260
But you actually did the thing like you hung
around for a while.

00:11:56.260 --> 00:12:01.910
Yeah, and I think what happens if I'm Jewish,
I'm also the grandson of two Holocaust survivors,

00:12:01.910 --> 00:12:05.920
and over time I would tell my subjects about
their terrible stories, you know, losing most

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:07.930
of their families.

00:12:07.930 --> 00:12:10.690
You know, I think everyone makes exceptions
for people they like.

00:12:10.690 --> 00:12:12.770
And the honest truth is that they came to
like me.

00:12:12.770 --> 00:12:15.130
They would text me and vulnerable moments.

00:12:15.130 --> 00:12:18.480
A lot of that that stuff that was on the record
is in the story I wrote about Lauren.

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:20.550
You know, they would make exceptions.

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:26.050
But the truth is, is that, like the Jew is
and always has been a stand in for any sort

00:12:26.050 --> 00:12:27.660
of issue that we see in society.

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:29.500
So now they're the bankers.

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:32.630
Soros, obviously, is something we use all
the time.

00:12:32.630 --> 00:12:36.480
He's the image of the parasite who's pulling
the strings from behind the scenes.

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:40.890
You know, it's very common to hear them talk
about the Rothschilds use to talk about the

00:12:40.890 --> 00:12:44.040
Zionist occupied government, which really
isn't anything about Israel.

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:47.930
It's actually saying that the US is and is
occupied by Jews behind the scenes.

00:12:47.930 --> 00:12:52.520
And, you know, I think there's so much dog
whistles, anti anti-Semitism that's all around

00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:53.520
in this movement.

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:57.310
Some of them don't even realize that they're
purveying it and others are actively doing

00:12:57.310 --> 00:12:58.310
it.

00:12:58.310 --> 00:13:02.000
And when it comes to the state, they're very,
very clear that that this might come as a

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:07.490
